{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1527341-masks-of-the-hierarchy","id":1527341,"title":"Masks of the Hierarchy","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-12-23T19:30:37-08:00","id":1527341,"title":"Masks of the Hierarchy","content":"I am interested in hearing ideas and descriptions of the various masks worn by members of the Hierarchy. I think it is an interesting detail of the society, but I've been disappointed at how few details we've been provided. They are supposed to be a &quot;fundamental element of wraith society&quot;, &quot;symbols of high station&quot;, &quot;extremely fashionable among many wraiths&quot; and &quot;depersonalize the office&quot;. Possessing a mask for an office that one does not have is supposed to be a crime punishable by discorporation, so it is very serious.<br /> <br /> I think to some degree this is supposed to allow us to use our imaginations and give us free reign. And I like that. However, 1) have examples would give me something to work with, and 2) if masks are used to represent offices, then it stands that even if they are individually different, the broad categories of ranks (Legionnaire, Centurion, Marshal, etc.) should have a style of mask that obviously denotes their status. If they are too individualistic, it's not obvious what rank in the Hierarchy one is.<br /> <br /> My idea is that there should be some continuity between any masks worn by the same rank, and some common motify for masks worn within the same Legion. Lower ranks would have more standardized masks, but they would become more individualistic as one goes up the ranks. Many of the Legions also have various orders and other sub-groups within them, and I imagine these have special masks designating their membership though these two might be distinguishable by rank.<br /> <br /> I imagine there are also &quot;private&quot; masks worn by ordinary citizens that are simply fashion accessories which may mark some kind of status, but which would not be confused with masks for actual office holders. Certain professions might also have common kinds of masks that indicate the type of service they provide (a merchant who deals in soul pathos crystals, someone skilled in castigate, someone who can safely escort you through the Tempest). These masks are probably regulated to a degree by the Hierarchy, and the wraith may have paid some kind of registration fee to obtain such a mask that is a symbol of some kind of professional competence. The origins of these kind of masks probably date to the Guilds.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-23T19:34:03-08:00","id":1527342,"title":"","content":"Some sourcebooks have given us some details. The Hierarchy sourcebook gave us some of the broad powers of the masks of the Deathlords, though not what they looked like. Still, I imagine it is a rare chronicle for one or more of the Deathlords actually make an appearance. I am more interested in the lower ranks as those wraiths are the ones PCs will likely encounter.<br /> <br /> The 1e corebook gives us some possible examples, though they don't explicitly say they are &quot;masks&quot;. One is the &quot;blindfold of justice&quot; a rank 2 artifact that covers the eyes of an &quot;Enforcer&quot;. We see this in several illustrations of the corebook. To me, this seems to be a common mask for Legionnaires. And we might see other Legionnaires who wear some kind of cloth mask that is fashioned around their head - kerchiefs, bandanas, face masks, scarves, keffiyehs, etc. - that can conceal their identity at least partially. One thing I like about the blindfold of justice is that it does have powers, which Hierarchy masks are supposed to have,<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, the one example we have of a possible Centurion (Jameson in Little Five Points, though he's not specifically called that) doesn't see to have a mask at all. Lost opportunity there.<br /> <br /> The only other thing that seems to correspond are the muzzles worn by barghests, but there are enslaved, moliated thralls so they aren't really masks. But we do have an evocative illustration from Josh Timbrook that shows the muzzle as clearly looking like the jaw of an animal, so I think they count as a mask. But for a lobotomized thrall, all muzzles are likely the same. We know braces of barghests are controlled by one or more &quot;keepers&quot;. Again frustratingly we're not told what actual rank a &quot;keeper&quot; is, but given their responsibility I assume they are specialized Centurions. I'd probably make them wear more elaborate masks covering their head that looks much like an animal head (say a wolf) that denotes their mastery of the barghests.<br /> <br /> After a (very) quick reread of the Hierarchy sourcebook, I can't find much written about masks. But the artwork certainly shows a lot. There are some who have very simple carnival / Venetian masks. Some masks that are overly large that extend way beyond the head, and full face covering masks that look monstrous. But I don't think there was any thought at providing some kind of system.<br /> <br /> I have not reread my other Wraith sourcebooks, but I don't remember any real details on masks by them. If anyone does know of references, I'd appreciate to know which books and their pages (if possible).<br /> <br /> Otherwise, I think this is something that just needs player ideas. So if you have any, or have seen any good examples during play, I'd love to hear them. For example, I think some group would probably have some plague doctor style masks. They are just so distinctive and rather cool looking. Don't know what group I'd assign them to yet though.<br /> <br /> Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-24T03:03:06-08:00","id":1527360,"title":"","content":"Forum devoured it","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","user":"Shadeprowler","date":". 12-29-2023, 01:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-24T03:04:11-08:00","id":1527361,"title":"","content":"Well, as usuall, for some reason the Forum ate it...<br /> Will delete the original as it not flaged anymore<br /> <br /> I think - and this is just my two obolus in the matter - the Masks and their meaning have their traition in Charon, but along the line, became warped by time<br /> Charon originally is from the ancient civilization of Mycenae<br /> The Mycenaean culture have a history with masks (and the Mycenaeans were influenced by the earlier Minoan civilization)<br /> The death masks of Mycenae, a series of golden funerary masks found on buried bodies within the ancient Greek city of Mycenae are famous<br /> The masks were likely direct representations of the deceased, symbolizing a continuation of the dead's identity in death, similar to funerary statues and incisions, immortalizing an idealized depiction of the deceased - but strangely funerary masks like the ones found at Mycenae have not been found anywhere else in Mycenaean culture<br /> On the other hand? There is also a death mask found in Grave Circle B, but it differs from the Circle A masks both in material, as it is made from electrum (not gold) and placement, as it was placed in a container besides an entombed body, rather than being placed upon the deceased.<br /> The relative scarcity of death masks in another grave in the city indicate that the buried dead were of lesser wealth or status, rather than the ones with the golden mask with an abundance of material made from valuable materials, such as the Death masks themselves<br /> The Mycenaeans used three main types of tombs: shaft tombs, chamber tombs, and tholos tombs. Shaft tombs are the earliest type of tomb which are rectangular graves cut into the bedrock. Chamber tombs are designed to look like houses, with an entrance passage (<i>dromos</i>), an entrance (<i>stomion</i>), and a chamber. Tholos tombs were similar to chamber tombs, but they were larger and their walls were built using corbelling. This technique gave tholos tombs a beehive shape.<br /> The Mycenaeans buried their dead with grave goods that show the deceased individual's social status. Elite tombs included gilded weapons, masks, jewelry, tools, and vessels made of rich materials like gold, amber, and silver. The grave goods in lower-class burials were not made with these luxury materials.<br /> <br /> These thing - safe to say again - was carried to the Afterlife and been the building blocks of the society Charon built<br /> <br /> Now, in Wraith Masks are soulforged artifacts worned by wraiths on their faces, typically for protection, disguise, performance, or entertainment.<br /> Wraiths in Stygia wear masks manly in order to hide their original identity. That what I ment by the warping of the funerary practices and beliefes of the Mycenaeans, the Mask became the opposites basically in a sense<br /> On the other hand?<br /> It is quite understandable.... a society where Moliation is common, Masks are &quot;fix&quot; points, thus ideal to be used to <i>depersonalize</i> offices (and/or Wraiths). The Masks get passed down to succesive holders, up to the Death Lords themselves<br /> As a consequence of this, many officials have separate identities when masked and unmasked - and the same office may be occupied by different individuals without public acknowledgement of such.<br /> The Ferrymen themselves do not wear masks, but instead wear their hoods over their faces to hide their identities - but before that, before the Ferrymen were banished, the Navigatus of the Boatmen's Society would wear a small mask to indicate solidarity with the Hierarchy<br /> <br /> Now I do think however, even tho' offices are tied to the Mask, not the person, on personal level, Wraiths indeed could use them to remain &quot;themselves&quot;<br /> After all Death Masks were popularly used in to recognize and honor the dead and to ensure their safety in the afterlife.<br /> The death mask had several purposes. A common one throughout the history of death masks was to help people remember the deceased individual by preserving their likeness. Before the invention of photography, a death mask was a common memento of a dead person.<br /> <br /> So, from these ancient burial customs I think evolved the usage of the Masks in the Hierarchy. One part to help the Wraiths to remain themselves in a sense and to depersonalize them in the meantime, tying them to be something not someone - and easier to control<br /> This is a bit like with the Masks of the Harbringers of Skull<br /> Khurshid is the wearer of the Sun Mask - witch is fitting, he was a Persian warlord of Sasanian Empire in life as well as death and the Sun always been a war symbol, thus this shows Khurshid is the de-facto warleader of the Harbringers, but in the meantime, if you wear the Sun Mask, in a sense, regardless who were you, you will be (the) Khurshid<br /> Erebus is the wearer of the Pantalone Mask - again, similar thing we can observe; Erebus was an oracle and administrator but Erebus and his fellow conspirators played a dangerous game, they seek an opportune time to drive the Baronies of Avalon into civil war and simultaneous conflict with outside domains, knowing their cause will thrive on a great number of vampire deaths. Erebus steadily pushes the domain down a chaotic path. And Just as the origins of many other masks, Pantalone\u2019s roots date back to the Latin comedy, namely to the character of the old man. During the 16th century, indeed, he was a rich old man, though greedy, and with a weakness for young women. This made him an enemy of the young, as well as a laughing stock, but he\u2019s was the guardian of traditions, as well, and, as such, of Venice, which he sees slowly sinking into corruption.<br /> <br /> The Mask are not really explained, sadly, but as you said this gives us a little freedom to use them as we see fit in a Wraith game<br /> When you pick up a mask, you became assosiated with the mask, you became the Wearer of the said Mask<br /> <br /> With the Death Mask angle, this could tremendously help a Wraith to cling to their human life, remember who they were, how they looked like - not to mention make it harder to impersonate you; any talented Wraith could &quot;steal your face&quot;, but to copy your Mask?<br /> Now thats hard (and more often than not, punishable)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","user":"Shadeprowler","date":". 12-25-2023, 04:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-25T19:44:52-08:00","id":1527448,"title":"","content":"I found some canonical mask descriptions in the Book of Legions. There's clearly no sense of theme or reason, just individual writers wanting to write a &quot;cool&quot; description. But I think they can work for the purpose of inspiration.<br /> <br /> The Warriors of Lethe (basically a special order of suicide bombers of the Silent Legion) wear featureless masks of midnight blue.<br /> <br /> The Legion of Onyx Tears are the bodyguards of the Beggar Lord, Legion of Paupers. They wear tall, black masks with stylized weeping eyes and laughing mouths.<br /> <br /> The beastmasters (keepers of barghests) of the Beast Corps of the Penitent Legion wear helmets with stylized hound's heads with bared teeth.<br /> <br /> The soldiers of the Grim Legion wear heavy helmets of a stylized human skull.<br /> <br /> The Laughing Lady wears a huge mask with an enormous toothy grin that looks like a maw going to bite.<br /> <br /> The Smiling Lord wears a skeletal mask.<br /> <br /> Not much to go on, but we have descriptions of two of the masks of the eight Deathlords. We know two special orders have their own special masks, which makes sense. Other special orders likely have their own unique masks as well. The mask of the keepers of barghests match what I expected earlier. The only thing that seems off is that ordinary Legionnaires of the Grim Legion would have outright helmets. I see that more as being a thing for Centurions.<br /> <br /> There does seem to be a theme that the Grim Legion masks involve a skull. While that makes some sense, it would seem more appropriate for the Skeletal Legion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-26T04:14:30-08:00","id":1527458,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1527341-masks-of-the-hierarchy?p=1527361#post1527361\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Charon originally is from the ancient civilization of Mycenae</div> </div> </div> </div>If the use of masks predates the foundation of the Hierarchy (12th century), their origins as death masks is pretty plausible. From there, it's possible that the mask evolved in style to those of classic Greek theater, and reflected the 'role' of the Wraith in society. The Legions are older than the Hierarchy, so the latter could simply have picked up that fashion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1527341-masks-of-the-hierarchy?p=1527342#post1527342\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are some who have very simple carnival / Venetian masks.</div> </div> </div> </div>I personally think Venetian masks were the core inspiration here. Two points of interest: the use of masks predates the carnival tradition, and their use in Venice almost coincides with the foundation of the Hierarchy. Their main function was to disguise the identity of a wearer of upper social status and allow them to indulge in all kinds of 'uncouth' activities. The side-effect of dissolving social boundaries only appeared later with the inclusion in the carnival.<br /> <br /> If we go with that speculative timeline, we end up with something that provides a degree of anonymity while still codifying the social status of the wearer. That code has probably evolved into something very abstract that would be hard for outsiders to pick up on, but easy to recognize for a Wraith integrated in the society like with all fashion - and as a consequence, may not seem directly related to the actual station of the wearer from an outside (Player) perspective. That could at least be used as a rationalization for the 'cool' designs that favor style over overt symbolism.<br /> <br /> When I described them to players, I went with a 'Dave McKean take on Venetian masks' just for the aesthetic. Fashion-coding is so abstract that any little detail could mean anything, so I think the ST can be free with the degree of symbolism they want to include.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-26T16:21:08-08:00","id":1527470,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1527341-masks-of-the-hierarchy?p=1527458#post1527458\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I personally think Venetian masks were the core inspiration here.</div> </div> </div> </div>In 1e Wraith corebook there is at least one picture of a wraith with a Venetian mask. The art in that sourcebook is very good and evocative. But there are only a few images with obvious Hierarchy style masks. It's a shame we couldn't have more pictures of Hierarchs with masks.<br /> <br /> I am inclined to agree with you that Venetian style masks do work best. They look great, can have a wide variety of looks despite having a very simple design, and are already culturally associated with influential secret societies or the rich and powerful in general.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GDjjXw-ezDU\" data-code=\"GDjjXw-ezDU\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/GDjjXw-ezDU/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DO0cW-ckrQw\" data-code=\"DO0cW-ckrQw\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/DO0cW-ckrQw/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mukaw49NGd0\" data-code=\"Mukaw49NGd0\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/Mukaw49NGd0/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5_lVqP1-Efo\" data-code=\"5_lVqP1-Efo\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/5_lVqP1-Efo/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> There is also enough <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carnival_of_Venice#Carnival_masks\" target=\"_blank\">variety to Venetian masks</a> that one can individualize them to some degree for NPCs.<br /> <br /> So I am likely to go with Venetian style masks for the basic ranks of the hierarchy, at least for Marshal/Inspector, Regent/Minister, Overlord/Chancellor (both military and civil), and the civil (non-military) ranks for Legionnaire and Centurion (Clerks and Adjustors). Military legionnaires and centurions need something more utilitarian and martial (and intimidating). The masks are relatively simple at lower ranks and become more elaborate as they gain status. It also means that at low levels of the Hierarchy, you can probably identify the wraith despite the mask, but as you go up they become more anonymous.<br /> <br /> The few pictures we have of Anacreon masks tend to be elaborate and not based on Venetian masks, so I'll likely keep those as such. And the Deathlords have individually unique masks and outfits so they will be completely anonymous.<br /> <br /> The more bizarre and outlandish masks I'll keep for the special orders of the Legions, those groups that are report directly to Charon (like the Unlidded Eye (the secret police) and the Magisterium Veritas (inquisitors to uncover Heretics), and regulated professions that came out of the Guilds. The Ferryman have their hoods that cover their faces. And ordinary civilians have masks they use that do not correspond to any official masks - there must be some sumptuary laws in Stygia that regulate them just so there is no confusion with<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1527341-masks-of-the-hierarchy?p=1527458#post1527458\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When I described them to players, I went with a 'Dave McKean take on Venetian masks' just for the aesthetic. Fashion-coding is so abstract that any little detail could mean anything, so I think the ST can be free with the degree of symbolism they want to include.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that's fine as a general statement. But sometimes I like to use more specifics to set the mood so coming up with common motifs and themes for the Legions or other groups is useful to me.<br /> <br /> ****<br /> I should also note that Venetian masks are used a lot in the comic The Marquis by Guy Davis. I've stolen a whole lot of stuff from that comic for Wraith, and Guy, of course, was once a big art contributor to the World of Darkness. I definitely recommend the comic to anyone who is interested, though I think all the collections are now quite old and Guy hasn't published any new series in many years.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 12-26-2023, 07:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-12-28T17:32:07-08:00","id":1527583,"title":"","content":"After doing some research on Venetian masks, I have some ideas on a general scheme of how I want to portray the Hierarchy. For those who want to follow some of the terms I'm about to use, I think <a href=\"https://magicofvenezia.com/history-of-venetian-masks/\" target=\"_blank\">this URL can provide the basics</a>. <a href=\"https://thecreativeadventurer.com/guide-to-venetian-carnival-masks/\" target=\"_blank\"> Another good URL is here</a>. The below is subject to change, and it is not intended as a straightjacket, just some general guidelines for my chronicles.<br /> <br /> As I've already mentioned, inspired by the Blindfold of Justice artifact, I am thinking of limiting the rank of Legionnaire/Clerk to cloth or lace masks. That might be blindfolds, scarves, hoods, veils, shawl, or anything else that could be used to cover the face. Very limited powers as artifacts, mostly limited to perception to help out the patrols.<br /> <br /> Centurions will have helms to provide protection to them in combat. They lead patrols and are the ones who need to get their hands dirty. Various types of helms are possible, but in order to effectively function as masks they either need to cover the face in full, or obscure most of it like the Corinthian helmet. No helms that are open faced or just cover the top of the head. For the civil equivalent of Adjustors, I'm not sure yet. Helmets wouldn't be appropriate for them. Maybe a mask of a helmet similar to oldstyle goaltender masks (think Jason in Friday the 13th, not modern hockey masks) or ballistic masks - something very utilitarian looking, but could still be decorated.<br /> <br /> Marshals/Inspectors are where we start seeing use of the Venetian style masks. They'll have some variant of the classic white volto/larva mask. Easy to elaborate on to make many different varieties.<br /> <br /> Regents/Ministers use some form of the bauta mask usually combined with a hat, hood, or cape of sometime. It's beak like chin easily distinguishes it from the volto style mask. It looks a bit more imposing.<br /> <br /> Overlords/Chancellors use more full face masks, often of animals or legendary beings. Such masks really don't show up in Venice, but are popular in Carnivals in other cities. Alternatively, the dama mask is also used, distinguished from the bauta and volto by being gilded and enameled instead of white.<br /> <br /> Anacreons have very elaborate full head masks/helms. They often incorporate chains, another motif of the Hierarchy that often does not get utilized. This is entirely due to the picture for Governor James Johnson in Necropolis: Atlanta which is the only character portrait that has a mask.<br /> <br /> The various half-masks worn at Carnival, based on the commedia dell'arte, are typically used by freewraiths. Unlike full masks, the half masks aren't regulated by the Hierarchy's sumptuary laws because they won't be confused by the masks the Hierarchy actually uses.<br /> <br /> The plague doctor masks with their long, beaked noses are only used by the Magisterium Veritas, the secret police of Stygia that owe loyalty directly to Charon. The grim riders of the Order of the Unlidded Eye, of course, wear knightly helms with visors.<br /> <br /> The Silent Legion is one exception. They use the moretta style mask which has no hole in the mask to speak through. Many masks of this style did not have ties around the head, but was held in place by the wearer biting on a button or bit in her mouth. The reason, of course, is that it's the Silent Legion. At low ranks, this might simply be decoration. But at higher ranks, the masks might actually require this preventing the wraith from talking, but of course provide the ability to communicate via thought or perhaps in a disembodied voice. Of course, the voice itself is unique to the mask and does not reflect the wraith's actual voice. I am thinking whether this should extend to the legionnaires where they would wear a cloth gag as well.<br /> <br /> Likewise the Penitent Legion, which serves the Laughing Lady, incorporates some kind of jester or jolly design in their masks. Laughing mouths and bells are very common.<br /> <br /> I'm still thinking of good motifs for each of the legions. Though I am considering that the Legion of Fate would have more Greek style theatre masks though still the size of the Venetian style masks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-09T05:05:16-08:00","id":1528256,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the info, I appreciate it <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28047-lewisdavis","name":"LewisDavis","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-09T21:37:27-08:00","id":1528300,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28047-lewisdavis\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28047\" data-vbnamecard=\"28047\">LewisDavis</a>, thanks. But if you have any ideas or comments you could add, I'd appreciate it more. This is such a wide open area, any new ideas and inspirations would be welcomed. There's so few ideas provided by the source material.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-18T17:00:22-08:00","id":1528845,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1527341-masks-of-the-hierarchy?p=1527583#post1527583\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">After doing some research on Venetian mask....</div> </div> </div> </div>On a related note, the Hecata are described as using masks extensively and being involved in the Venetian carnival.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-04-23T11:24:55-07:00","id":1550340,"title":"","content":"I found another canon description of Hierarchy masks so as a public service I am updating this thread. The Buried Secrets sourcebook lists Masks as a level 1 soulsteel artifact. We get some more details.<br /> <br /> Official Hierarchy masks are made from polished soulsteel; each forged from a single traitor's soul.<br /> <br /> Their only artifact &quot;power&quot; is that they adjust to fit any wearer not extensively marred by Moliation. Otherwise most masks are strictly functional and decorative.<br /> <br /> Anacreon masks look like heraldic skulls.<br /> <br /> It also confirms that barghest trainers have masks with a distinctly houndlike feature, which we've previously discussed.<br /> <br /> I'd probably have this mask available at Centurion rank and above, but not used for the typical legionnaire.<br /> <br /> There is also a level 2 artifact weapon called Soulfire Mask. These are Stygian steel masks designed for high ranking Hierarchy officials for protection. Their &quot;grimacing visages&quot; have a soulfire gem. Upon activation the Soulfire Mask either produces spewing ribbons of flame that attacks someone, or surrounds the mask wearer with a nimbus of flames that absorbs physical damage. Since Anacreon masks are listed under the normal Masks artifact, these Soulfire Masks are probably given only to those high officials who are especially favored for past service or perhaps considered to be specially targeted and need more protection, not just given out. I imagine they are distinctive in and of themselves because of the presence of the soulfire gem. That might be to discourage an attempt in the first place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}